WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Let me just tell you what's going on.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.000
You have it?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Which pages?

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.000
You have about less than an hour to study.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
It's my fault, I know, because I should have been here early, but...

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Take longer, okay?

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.000
All right.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Now, just let me tell you what's going on here.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:39.000
People who are literate, who went to school, went to college, went to university, and then they read the Torah, they read the Bible.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.000
In English or Latin, whatever your language happens to be.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.000
And then you see the Bible, and then you see what the law, the halacha, requires of you.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.000
And there's a discrepancy.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.000
The Bible seems to be very simple.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Yes?

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Don't work on the Sabbath.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.000
People say, don't work on the Sabbath, you don't go to your office, or whatever it is.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.000
All of a sudden, it's like a match, or you put on a line, or you sign your initials.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.000
And someone says, oh, that's haram because you work on Shabbat.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.000
It doesn't make sense.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:20.000
So, the problem with Judaism, and that's why you see here the greatness of this hakam.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.000
The problem with Judaism is not the Bible.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Yes?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Even Christians, even Muslims, respect the Bible.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
So, what's the problem with Judaism?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
It's that our interpretation of the Bible seems different than anybody's interpretation.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
It's what we call the halacha, the Jewish law.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:47.000
The practice of Judaism doesn't seem to jive with the Bible.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.000
All right?

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Now, again, we're talking about a society.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.000
It was highly educated.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Rabbi David Nieto was the chief rabbi of the Sephardic community in England.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Not only was this a very educated society, but also there was a psychological warfare going on.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.000
What was the psychological warfare?

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:22.000
There were Jews in Portugal and Spain who attained extraordinary achievements in literature and science and commerce.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:29.000
And we were trying to attract them because they were still living, pretending to be Christians in Spain.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Some of these people we were able to attract, and they came and they joined our communities.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:43.000
But these people, when they came to join our communities, all of a sudden, because they were alienated for 200, 300 years from Judaism,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.000
they were uncomfortable.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Yes. All right?

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:55.000
And therefore there was a need to explain the mechanism by which we interpret the Bible.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.000
We interpret everything.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.000
The thesis of this hacham, which is my thesis.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.000
I defend this in two of my books.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Ionim b'mishet ra'alei al-anbar, which is in Hebrew.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.000
I defended this thesis through halacha.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:15.000
And another book, which is in English, is Golden Doves, which is a textuality in Semitic and Rabbinic literature.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.000
The thesis is the following.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.000
We have a tradition.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Going back, I tell you the truth, I believe it is primozaic.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.000
According to Abraham Avinu.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.000
Yes. Of course, the most authentic part is that comes from Moshe Rabbeinu.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.000
But it comes from, like the milah.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.000
The milah comes from Abraham.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.000
So there are ancient, very ancient traditions.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.000
That goes even to Noah.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:47.000
I mean, I believe that there is no society, that our society, the Sephardim, the Jewish society in general,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.000
we are the recipients of the oldest tradition of civilized man.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Could be even from Noah.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Could be even from pre-Noah.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.000
That's my thesis.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.000
And we interpret what we interpret.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Because of our tradition.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Our tradition conditions the way we look into the Bible.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:12.000
So in a sense, it is, we interpret, we are what we are because of our tradition.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.000
The Bible, we project our tradition in the Bible.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Now, for instance, here is going to, this hakam is acting now as a devil's advocate.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.000
He's presenting the thesis of those who are against us.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
Protesting.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.000
All right?

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
What seems unfair or unreasonable in the rabbinic tradition.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.000
Example.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.000
In Chav Gimal, if you want to see to the bottom.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Katu Bat Torah.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.000
We read it once, but it's so nice that I think it pays to read it again.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Katu Bat Torah.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Chav Gimal at the bottom.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Lo tashhit et pe'at zeh kanecha.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.000
Do not shave the side of your beard.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Now, it doesn't say lo tegaleah.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.000
Lo tashhit.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.000
Yes.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.000
All right?

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Now, u'mashma mea katuv.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.000
Now the word mashma here is very precise.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.000
It means a superficial reading of the basuk will show.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.000
But the word mashma means superficial.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.000
Yes?

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.000
Shen yisr erav et ve'ah hat.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.000
That only is haram one side.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.000
Actually we have five sides.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:22.000
This side, this side, this side, this side, this side.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.000
All right?

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Two on top.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.000
Two here and here.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.000
All right?

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.000
Five.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.000
The Torah does not say lo tashhit.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.000
Pe'at zeh kanecha.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.000
Mashma.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.000
Ahad.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.000
One.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.000
Now, qabla haqameen want to say it's haram five.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:47.000
If you shave your whole face, you make five abunod.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.000
This is from the Wahhabis.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.000
At least now this haqameen is acting as the devil's advocate.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.000
All right?

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.000
Ve'lor ne'l-parash ve'l-torah, ezo hiya pe'ah.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.000
The Torah doesn't say which side.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.000
Now the Torah doesn't say lo teqalea.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.000
It says lo tashhit.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.000
Do not destroy.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Ve'khilo ne'l-parash imash hatayi ve'kol mine keleem.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.000
The Torah also doesn't say with which instrument.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Maybe any instrument with which you shave is haram.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.000
Scissor.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.000
An electric shaver.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.000
Powder.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Waxing.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Or be'miqsat.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.000
Or maybe it's only haram with some ways.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Now come the haqameen.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.000
And they make the following principle.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.000
Hei me'khilo ve'keleem.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.000
In concerning the instrument with which you shave,

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.000
they made it very easy.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.000
They say only it's haram with a razor.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Now if you shave yourself with a scissor,

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.000
although it could be the scissor of a surgeon,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.000
ke'in tahar,

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.000
it is permitted.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Even ke'in tahar,

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.000
even if the scissor would shave you

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:08.000
as well as a blade, as a razor,

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.000
still the scissor is mutar, is permitted,

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.000
and the razor haram.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.000
It makes no sense.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.000
I mean, that's the question.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.000
Now let me explain to you, because again,

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.000
when I was a child,

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.000
there was in Argentina a rabbi who was not,

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.000
really was not a rabbi.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.000
And he said that even it's haram to shave

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.000
even with an electric shaver.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.000
The truth is that he was wrong,

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.000
to put it mildly.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.000
Because he said, ah, because it changes,

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:39.000
it changes better.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.000
He says, you know, touch it.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:45.000
It feels better than a razor.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.000
So for this haram, who was not really a haram,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.000
he was against shaving with a razor.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.000
One second.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.000
But the truth is that there's a difference.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.000
Scissor, the difference of scissor

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:03.000
is something that you catch the hair,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.000
and then you cut it over another side.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.000
That is what it is.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.000
The same principle functions with a razor.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:14.000
You need the cover of the razor.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.000
The cover of the razor acts like the other side of the scissor.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.000
Electric razor.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.000
Of the electric razor.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.000
You understand?

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.000
Now, with a regular razor,

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.000
it cuts you against the flesh.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.000
It catches the hair against the flesh.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.000
The scissor.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.000
Or an electric razor.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.000
Yes.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.000
It does it with another thing.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.000
Yes.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.000
That is the difference.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.000
So it makes a difference how well it shaves.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.000
When I use a shaver,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.000
you know, after I shave,

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.000
I look like a perfectly well-shaven.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.000
Although there is no hair,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.000
and maybe even less than with a regular blade,

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.000
it is permitted.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.000
Because it was done with...

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.000
Two.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.000
Two.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.000
And although it's ke'en ta'ar,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.000
ke'en ta'ar means it shaves as well as a blade,

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.000
still one thing is permitted,

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.000
and the other thing is forbidden.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.000
Rocky, you raise your hand.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.000
Obviously, every man has a beard.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.000
Yes.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:16.000
And so which culture adopted the practice of not having a beard,

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:21.000
and what did the Jews try to emulate this alien culture,

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.000
and the second part of the question,

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.000
are there certain Jewish people today who don't have,

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:29.000
who have beards because they're trying to make some other kind of...

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:30.000
Let me, let me, yeah.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Very, very briefly.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:33.000
It's a book.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.000
Yes.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:35.000
No, no, very briefly.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:39.000
The Romans, they were shaven.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:43.000
And people who were, there were other cultures,

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:47.000
like the Germans, who did not shave.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:51.000
And these are called, in Latin, barbarians.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.000
Barbarian comes from the word barba,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.000
which means a beard,

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.000
and that you call a barber a barber.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.000
It's a Latin word.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.000
Barber.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:00.000
I'm a linguist.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.000
I'm a linguist.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.000
That's why, you know, I could make this kind of correction.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.000
I know that now.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.000
You know, all right.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.000
So now, the Romans were the first one.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.000
All right.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.000
We Jews, we're not in favor of growing a beard.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.000
The Torah doesn't say.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.000
The Torah is not shy.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.000
The Torah wants you to grow a beard.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:16.000
The Torah says, and you should grow a beard.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.000
No.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.000
The Torah doesn't say, don't grow a beard.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:20.000
The Torah says,

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.000
lo tashhit biadzikanecha.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.000
Do not destroy it with destruction.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:26.000
That's the word, tashhit.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.000
The hakamim define the word tashhit as ta'an.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.000
Ta'an means a blade.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:32.000
All right.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:35.000
Now, the other people want to grow a beard.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.000
God bless them.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.000
It's not haram.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.000
But my teachers, both in Argentina,

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:42.000
Hakam Eliyahu Freire,

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:43.000
Hakam Eliyahu Suli,

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.000
Hakam Eliyahu, what's his name,

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.000
I forgot his first name,

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.000
Shilomo,

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:50.000
Ajami,

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.000
all the hakamim,

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.000
none of them had a beard.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:54.000
Hakam Matlubi here in this country,

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:55.000
didn't have a beard.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.000
They didn't have a beard.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.000
All right.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.000
Why?

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.000
Because the question is showing off.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.000
We don't like to show off.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:02.000
That's the truth.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.000
You know.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.000
Beard is not musfat to grow a beard.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:08.000
Ah, in Syria, everybody had a beard.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.000
Because that was part of the fashion of the country.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:11.000
You see.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:13.000
So it's ridiculous to try to

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.000
to superimpose the fashion of Syria in America.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:17.000
That's what it says.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:18.000
You see.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:19.000
But...

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:20.000
What?

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.000
The rabbis in Syria didn't grow beards.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:23.000
In the old times, yes.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.000
Now, maybe, but in the old times,

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:26.000
I tell you,

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.000
there was also, not only the rabbis,

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:30.000
most people there,

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.000
because it was very difficult to shave.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.000
Only people who were in Europe.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.000
Yes.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.000
And therefore, in Europe,

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:39.000
it was hyped to be with a beard.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:40.000
They did it.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.000
When I was a child,

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:45.000
I used to shave with a powder.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:48.000
It was a special kind of powder

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.000
that I used to shave with.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.000
That's the powder.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:52.000
You see.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:53.000
And by the way,

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.000
I want to say something,

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.000
and then let me continue.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:56.000
People didn't shave,

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.000
even to go in,

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.000
more than once or twice a week.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.000
That was...

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.000
This, that you shave every day,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.000
that was in the last 50 years.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.000
Even, you know, like,

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:08.000
I'm going to say,

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:09.000
but it's possible

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:11.000
that even the President of the United States,

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.000
you know, senators,

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.000
did not shave every day.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:16.000
No shower.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.000
No shower every day.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:19.000
That's the truth.

